### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

For the following test (which came from a reduction of a crash of a live site when reloading)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<script>
if (performance.getEntriesByType("navigation")[0].type !== "reload") {
  location.reload();
  history.replaceState(null, "", "?replaced");
} else {
  navigation.currentEntry;
}
</script>
```

Here E0 refers to the current entry when entering the test.

- location.reload() queues apply the history step
- replaceState() creates E1 and queues finalize a same-document navigation (entryToReplace = E0) as a sync navigation
- Apply the history step step 8.1: targetEntry = E0.
- Apply the history step step 14.1.1 lets [finalize a same-document navigation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#finalize-a-same-document-navigation) jump the queue and step 5 replaces E0 with E1.
- Apply the history step step 14.5 still has targetEntry = E0 while step 14.9 gives entriesForNavigationAPI = [E1], so update document for history step application step 6.5.3 calls [initialize the navigation API entries for a new document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#initialize-the-navigation-api-entries-for-a-new-document) with newSHEs = [E1], initialSHE = E0
- Step 5 of that initialisation leaves index at −1.

Now [navigation.currentEntry assertion](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#navigation-current-entry) fails on Assert: navigation's [current entry index](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#navigation-current-entry-index) is not −1.

The above steps deterministically result in spec assertion failure and session history to end up in a different state to what implementations do.
